From: j7
Subject: j7: scale the panel row straight into the back buffer

With the double buffer on, present() paints a cacheable back buffer, but scaledCopy() still
built every panel row in a small staging array and then memcpy()ed it 4,320 bytes to where it
belongs.  That is a second pass over 5.7 MB per frame - the whole panel read out of the cache and
written back again - for a row that was already complete when the loop that builds it ended.

When the destination is that back buffer (fFlip), scale straight into it, and leave the staging
row and its copy to the cases that write the write-combined panel buffer, where a per-pixel store
is still the wrong thing to do.  The duplicate-row skip is untouched: the row it would have
reused was written into this same back buffer moments earlier, so it is still correct.

Expected in the next log: `copy split build_us` down and `avg_copy_us` below the ~14 ms this
panel copy has cost since the double buffer went in.

Co-Authored-By: dipa-studio <183933817+dipa-studio@users.noreply.github.com>

